  "question": {
    "text": "Number of optical isomers possible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> – chlorobutane ........",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The number of optical isomers possible for a compound is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></msup></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo></math> number of asymmetric carbon atoms. </p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/a68bb1e5-c07f-4472-b1a5-adfd7278b131.png\" style=\"width: 228px; height: 171px;\"/></p><p>As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=1</mo></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><mi>chlorobutane</mi></math>, it is an optically active molecule with one chiral carbon atom and it forms dextro and laevo isomers.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>. </p><p>Hence, it has two optical isomers.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/7b260fd7-5c6a-4aff-8e15-110fde44f205.png\" style=\"width: 330px; height: 122px;\"/></p>",
